  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Minimal impacts Downeast from the coastal storm\ntoday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nModels are in general agreement that low pressure will track\nsouth of the Gulf of Maine to east of Nova Scotia today. The \nnorthern edge of the precipitation shield with the low will \nclip the Downeast region this morning with a chance of sow and \nrain. Little, or no, additional snow accumulation is expected. \nAny precipitation should end later this morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is the potential for snow showers across \nthe North Saturday through Sunday, with accumulation possible \nacross higher elevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper low will move south toward the region tonight, then\ncross the forecast area Saturday. The low will bring\nisolated/scattered snow and rain showers to mostly\nnorthern/northwest portions of the forecast area along with\nthe west-central highlands. The snow showers could possibly\nproduce light accumulations, generally less than an inch, across\nmostly higher terrain areas. \n\nThe closed upper level low exits to the south Saturday night, \nfollowed by an upper level shortwave passing from south to north\nduring the day on Sunday in its wake. These two systems could \nbring some isolated to scattered snow showers to mainly the \nNorth Saturday night and Sunday (with rain or snow showers \nSunday afternoon across portions of Eastern Aroostook, and \nNorthern Penobscot). While any accumulations should generally be\nat most a few tenths of an inch, any stronger snow showers on \nSunday could produce a quick inch or two across elevations above\n1000 ft. The best chance for this would be for areas along and \nwest of Route 11 north of Ashland and across Central and \nNorthern Piscataquis County.",
